WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.550
Follow one set of lines
in hydrogen's spectrum,

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.950
and they crowd together
until they reach a limit.

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.850
That limit is an electron falling
all the way from infinity,

4
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.490
from completely outside the atom.

5
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.770
Run it backwards, and the same energy
pulls the electron right out of the atom.

6
00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.990
So how much energy does it take to
pull an electron out of an atom?

7
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.710
And is it the same for every element?

8
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.350
In this mission, you'll turn that
limit into kilojoules per mole,

9
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.710
and follow the pattern of first ionization
energies across the periodic table.

10
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.370
Then you'll use successive ionization
energies to find an element's group.

11
00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:43.470
Your mission: find out how much energy
it takes to remove an electron, and why.
